WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.700
A real circulant Hadamard matrix of
positive integer order n exists if

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.900
and only if n is one or four. We finish
both directions of this statement:

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.580
exclude every other order, and verify
that the two remaining orders occur.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.940
Suppose first that n is greater than one.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.360
The order restriction from Node Two
gives n equal to four u squared,

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.940
where u is a positive odd integer.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.460
The general contradiction
excludes every u greater than one.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.340
Thus u equals one, and n equals four.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.760
The order-one case is outside the
hypothesis n greater than one of

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.980
that restriction. Keep it on a separate
path, and check its existence directly.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.320
At order one, take the
one-by-one matrix with entry one.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.500
Its entry is a sign, and its
only row has squared length one.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:56.160
There are no distinct rows to compare.
A cyclic shift fixes this single entry.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.080
Multiplying the matrix by its transpose
gives the one-by-one identity.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.600
This verifies the order-one example.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:10.576
At order four, take the matrix whose
first row is negative one, one, one, one.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.740
Each following row is the cyclic
right shift of the preceding row.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.980
This moves the negative sign
through all four positions.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.320
Any two distinct rows agree in
two positions and disagree in two.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.220
Their dot product is therefore two
positive ones plus two negative ones,

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.630
which is zero. Each row
has squared length four.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.760
So the matrix times its transpose has
fours on the diagonal and zeros elsewhere:

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.300
H times H transpose equals
four times the identity.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.560
Both remaining orders occur. The
order-four example has u equal to one,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.720
outside the earlier Delta theorem's
hypothesis u greater than one, so it is

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.560
consistent with the contradiction. This
completes both directions of the proof

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.780
chain, using the stated inputs
developed in the earlier nodes.
